{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_77d6552b789e2b9c702eac4aecafff7a79e2620ed044697ce805a47b1fd9f3ae", "title": "Pittsfield Colonials", "text": "The Pittsfield Colonials were a baseball team in the independent Canadian-American Association of Professional Baseball, based in Pittsfield, Massachusetts. The team was previously known as the American Defenders of New Hampshire (often simply referred to as the \"Defenders\"), and prior to that was known as the Nashua Pride.", "score": 22.4724}, {"id": "hp_doc_dcc55095979caf4a64b8f3c86b5d73218f02068427713721885e7ba0c7ed0787", "title": "Nashua Pride", "text": "The Nashua Pride were a professional baseball team based in Nashua, New Hampshire, in the United States, not affiliated with Major League Baseball. They played home games at Holman Stadium from 1998 through 2008, when they were sold and renamed the American Defenders of New Hampshire. In 2010 that team moved to Pittsfield, Massachusetts, and became the Pittsfield Colonials. The franchise itself no longer exists, as the Colonials folded after the 2011 baseball season.", "score": 19.2031}, {"id": "hp_doc_e4aafa0d72696805b427ac6ceaba7bcbdccf6642c2924ea459c35e6f550138f5", "title": "Pittsfield Red Sox", "text": "The Pittsfield Red Sox was the name of an American minor league baseball franchise based in Pittsfield, Massachusetts, from 1965 through 1969. It was the Double-A Eastern League affiliate in the Boston Red Sox farm system and produced future Major League Baseball players such as George Scott, Sparky Lyle, Reggie Smith and Hall of Famer Carlton Fisk. The team played at Wahconah Park.", "score": 14.4119}], "remaining_searches": 2}
